  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "Features\n\nMethods whose type parameter allows ref structs (#299, closes #201). A method with where T : allows ref struct (C# 13) no longer reports IMP009. A value of T may be a ref struct, so the imposter passes it through, whatever the type argument:\n\nSetups and verification take matchers for the other parameters only, as in imposter.Measure\\<Gauge>(Arg\\<int>.Any()).Returns((value, scale) => value.Level \\* scale).\n\nThe delegates passed to Returns, Callback and Throws, and the base implementation, get the value. A result of type T comes only from a Returns delegate or the base implementation.\n\nA setup applies to calls with the same type arguments only.\n\nThis needs the Roslyn 4.14 compiler or later (.NET SDK 9.0.300, Visual Studio 17.14). Older compilers still report IMP009 for such a method.\n\nThis completes #201. Still IMP009, and documented in Limitations:\n\na ref struct indexer key taken by in;\n\na class indexer of a ref struct type with an in or ref readonly key and a base getter;\n\na ref struct argument of an async event;\n\na span returned by reference (IMP011 covers ref returns).\n\nFixes\n\nInterface overloads that differ only in their type parameter count (#300, closes #298). void Use() next to void Use\\<T>() got numbered setups, so imposter.Use\\<int>() didn't compile; it had to be imposter.Use\\_1\\<int>(). Such overloads now keep their names.\n\nGeneric methods that name their type parameters differently (#300, closes #298). Two interfaces' Use\\<T>(T) and Use\\<U>(U), or a method hiding an inherited one under another type parameter name, failed with CS0111. They're now set up apart, through their interfaces' setup views.",
